  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter ItemDraw
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 ItemDraw: draw one collected item, whichever kind it turned out to be.

 THE TREE'S DETAIL IS DECIDED BY ITS NEIGHBOURS AND NOT BY ITS DISTANCE, which is
 the one interesting rule in the chapter. A crown is drawn as cones only if fewer
 than four collected trees are closer than this one -- so the nearest few get the
 expensive form however far away they all are, and a dense near cluster does not
 cost four times as much as a sparse one. Counting distance instead would look
 identical on a normal frame and fall apart in a grove.

 THE COMPARISON IS STRICTLY CLOSER, so a tree tied with another does not count it,
 and a tree is never closer than itself. Both matter at a boundary: four trees at
 the same depth all see three closer, and all four get cones.

 THE CROWN SHADE IS A CLAMPED RAMP over eighty metres, and both clamps are graded
 -- behind the eye it is full and past the range it is nothing, neither of which
 is reachable by reading the ramp alone.

 We say:

Section: The Distances

  fixed-got : List Real
  fixed-got = [crown-shade-dist, detail-dist]

  fixed-want : List Real
  fixed-want = [80.0, 200.0]

Section: Counting What Is Closer

  tree-at : Real -> TreeItem
  tree-at (fwd) = TreeItem { right = 0.0, fwd = fwd, height = 5.85, color = 1858082 }

  spread : List TreeItem
  spread = [tree-at 10.0, tree-at 20.0, tree-at 30.0, tree-at 40.0, tree-at 50.0]

  count-got : List Integer
  count-got =
    [ closer-count spread 10.0 0,
      closer-count spread 30.0 0,
      closer-count spread 50.0 0,
      closer-count spread 60.0 0,
      closer-count spread 5.0 0,
      closer-count [] 10.0 0 ]

  count-want : List Integer
  count-want = [0, 2, 4, 5, 0, 6 - 6]

 TIES DO NOT COUNT. Four trees at one depth each see three closer than
 themselves -- no, none: the test is strict, so each sees zero and all four are
 drawn in detail. That is the case a `<=` would break, and it is exactly a
 roadside pair at the same along.

  tied : List TreeItem
  tied = [tree-at 20.0, tree-at 20.0, tree-at 20.0, tree-at 20.0]

  tie-got : List Integer
  tie-got = [closer-count tied 20.0 0, closer-count tied 20.1 0, closer-count tied 19.9 0]

  tie-want : List Integer
  tie-want = [0, 4, 0]

Section: The Crown Shade

 A ramp from full at the eye to nothing at eighty metres, clamped at both ends.

  shade-got : List Real
  shade-got =
    [ crown-shade-of 0.0,
      crown-shade-of 20.0,
      crown-shade-of 40.0,
      crown-shade-of 80.0,
      crown-shade-of 200.0,
      crown-shade-of (0.0 - 50.0) ]

  shade-want : List Real
  shade-want = [1.0, 0.75, 0.5, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0]

Section: A Tower's Base

 The four corners are re-derived from the yaw at draw time rather than carried,
 which is why the collector kept the recipe. Four of them, whatever the yaw.

  segs : List Segment
  segs = [segment-at 0, segment-at 1, segment-at 2]

  chain : List Integer
  chain = [0, 1, 2]

  origin : Pose
  origin = Pose { along = 0.0, across = 0.0, yaw = 0.0, hw = 2.0 }

  a-tower : TowerItem
  a-tower = TowerItem { map = chain-map 0, a0 = 200.0, x0 = 22.0, yaw = 0.0, fwd = 200.0, off = 0.0 }

  base-got : List Integer
  base-got =
    [ list-length (tower-base segs chain origin a-tower 0),
      list-length (tower-base segs chain origin (TowerItem { map = chain-map 0, a0 = 200.0, x0 = 22.0, yaw = 1.0, fwd = 200.0, off = 0.0 }) 0) ]

  base-want : List Integer
  base-want = [4, 4]

 At zero yaw the square is axis-aligned and six metres each way, so its corners
 straddle the tower's centre in both axes. The rider is on the centre line, so
 the across values come out relative to him.

  corner-got : List Real
  corner-got =
    let b = tower-base segs chain origin a-tower 0
    in [ (list-at b 0).forward, (list-at b 2).forward,
         (list-at b 0).right, (list-at b 1).right ]

  corner-want : List Real
  corner-want = [194.0, 206.0, 14.0, 26.0]

Section: Drawing One

 A tower a long way off still draws; one behind the eye draws nothing, because
 every rod is near-clipped away.

  drawn : Real -> Integer
  drawn (a0) =
    list-length (draw-one-tower segs chain origin
      (TowerItem { map = chain-map 0, a0 = a0, x0 = 22.0, yaw = 0.0, fwd = a0, off = 0.0 }) 500.0 0.0)

  tower-got : List Boolean
  tower-got = [drawn 200.0 > 0, drawn 1000.0 > 0, drawn (0.0 - 100.0) == 0]

  tower-want : List Boolean
  tower-want = [True, True, True]

 THE FOUR NEAREST GET CONES AND THE FIFTH GETS FLAT TIERS -- and the two forms
 emit the SAME NUMBER of commands, one per tier plus the trunk, which is why the
 count cannot tell them apart. What differs is the shape: a flat tier is a
 triangle at its simplest, while a cone tier is the convex hull of a sixteen-point
 ring and carries more. The inequality is the assertion; the exact vertex count of
 a hull is the hull's business and TreeSpec grades it.

  cluster : List TreeItem
  cluster = [tree-at 10.0, tree-at 11.0, tree-at 12.0, tree-at 13.0, tree-at 14.0]

  tree-got : List Boolean
  tree-got =
    let near = draw-one-tree cluster 0 500.0
    in let far = draw-one-tree cluster 4 500.0
    in let near-tier = list-length (list-at near 1).pts
    in let far-tier = list-length (list-at far 1).pts
    in [list-length near > 0, list-length far > 0, near-tier > far-tier, far-tier >= 6]

  tree-want : List Boolean
  tree-want = [True, True, True, True]

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "id-fixed " fixed-got fixed-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "id-count " count-got count-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "id-tie   " tie-got tie-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "id-shade " shade-got shade-want 0.0)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints  "id-base  " base-got base-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-reals "id-corner" corner-got corner-want 0.0001)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "id-tower " tower-got tower-want)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "id-tree  " tree-got tree-want)
  end
